			<div class="content" id="post-content-4834326">
									

<blockquote>
<p>My main concern is the fact that you plan to spend a good chunk of the narrative describing how this alien race destroys themselves containing this ‘mass’, only for the ending to pivot and depict the mass as a god-chatting pacifist that wouldn’t harm a fly. To put it another way, it seems like there are two stories here, told one after the other.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Yea you've got a hell of a point. The reasoning for the mass' change is that it encountered another space-faring being that essentially &quot;taught&quot; the mass of exploration and &quot;the beauty of the universe&quot; so to speak. The relation is intended to be that the mass was like a child lashing out and needed to be taught morality. There is meant to be uncertainty as to if the mass is simply being manipulative or not. If it serves the narrative better I'll focus more on the transmissions.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I’m also a bit confused about the origin of the mass. You explicitly state that an activated latent gene causes it early on, then cap off your comment by suggesting Yaldabaoth caused it instead. Is this intentional? If so, why the ambiguity?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I worded badly. The activation of the gene is due to a technology based in sarkicism. Aliens mix sarkicism with technology and end up activating this gene. The gene &quot;unlocks a species true form&quot;, or at least that's what the mass ends up implying to the foundation. I do apologise if I failed to explain anything properly.</p>

